 ],
 "title": "Foundations of Probabilistic Programming",
 "byline": "Gilles Barthe, Joost-Pieter Katoen and Alexandra Silva (eds.) \u00b7 Cambridge University Press, 2020 (open access)",
 "note": "An edited volume of 15 contributed chapters. Introduction, conclusion and related-work sections are not broken out. Summaries are in our own words; the text stays in the PDF.",
 "summary": "A survey volume on the theory of probabilistic programs, from the semantics of languages with random choice and conditioning to methods for verifying and analyzing them. It covers measure-theoretic and computable semantics, probabilistic \u03bb-calculi, relational verification with couplings, expected-runtime calculi, termination via martingales, concentration-of-measure analysis, a logical/categorical account of Bayesian reasoning, quantitative equational reasoning, probabilistic abstract interpretation for privacy, quantitative information flow in Haskell, and three applied languages: Luck for property-based testing generators, Tabular for spreadsheet inference, and Rely for reasoning about unreliable hardware."
}